welcome to the Macmillan report I  Marilyn woke your host and today our  guest is Professor Paul Saban an  assistant professor of environmental  history professor Saban's research and  teaching focus on us environmental  history energy politics and political  and economic history including natural  resource development in the American  West and overseas his book crude  politics examines how politics and law  shaped growing dependence on petroleum  in California and the nation today we'll  talk with Professor Sabin about how  history can prepare us for the climate  crisis and energy transition welcome  professor savin Thank You maryland's a  pleasure to be here in your latest si  you argue that historians can help us  prepare for the climate and energy  crises give us your perspective sure  well in the most recent work I'm really  trying to figure out what role  historians might have to play in  addressing the climate problem and also  the energy issues and I come at it from  the perspective that we've been aware of  the climate problem for many years since  the there's a church of the jewels  Chinese report in 1979 so really the  last three decades we've really been  aware of the broad scope of the climate  problem but we haven't been able to  solve solve the problem and so in my  recent work I'm re i argue that one of  the reasons that this is the case is  that the problem has been addressed  primarily by lawyers and scientists and  engineers and economists and really that  the energy problem its nature is one  that is is not actually a technical  problem as a social problem and  historians have a lot to offer in  thinking about climate and energy in  that context and so on the essay I'm  exploring some of the insights of  history might bring to bear to the  climate problem and energy issues okay  so what are some of the lessons that  historians can offer us well I think the  first lesson is that is that history  really is embedded in a lot of the  conversations around climate change and  in in that that as people try to develop  solutions to these problems they're  they're using his  story yet at the same time historians  haven't been involved in the  conversation so some of the examples  might be for instance right now there's  a current debate in Congress about  whether to pursue a cap-and-trade bill  for for carbon dioxide emissions which  would set an overall cap for for these  emissions and then allow industries and  businesses to trade those underneath  those cap so that's one idea and then  the other is to pursue attacks that  would raise the price of carbon carbon  emissions and i think that the this  debate has been profoundly influenced by  historical interpretation of the early  1990s when the clinton administration  pursued an energy tax and i think ours  widely believed that the 1994 a  republican takeover of congress was was  in part a reaction to do this among  other things but about the get the  energy taxes were a significant factor  and by contrast the there were a series  of amendments the Clean Air Act under  the first Bush administration that  allowed for a kind of cap and trade  system that to deal with the problem of  acid rain and those were seen as a  widely seen as being successful and so I  think that this this historical analogy  is now being applied to the current  situation where although many economists  thinks that attacks would be a better  solution to the carbon dioxide emissions  problem it's seen as political suicide  and so sort of dead in the water okay so  you are seeing patterns particularly in  the political arena that may persist  moving forward or or not well I think  there are a lot there are a lot of  patterns and I guess I let me just  extend a little bit some of the ways in  which histories is being used first I  wanted to mention also that I think you  could you can make the argument that  that some of the reasons why climate  legislation hasn't moved forward in the  United States is that has to do with  some of the dominant historical myths so  I think this is another historical a  role for historians as to address some  of these myths and one of those is the  is the idea that the energy market is a  free market and that the dominance of  fossil fuels in the economy today is  Israel Israel  alt of just free competition among  industries and and fuels over the last  century and I think a historical  perspective allows us to see that the  role of the role the government played  over the last hundred years whether it  was in tax policy or questions over  property rights or also regulation if  you think of the role the Texas Railroad  Commission in ascending energy setting  oil prices in many ways in the United  States so I think 11 contribution  historians can make is the is really to  explode the idea that there is a free  market and energy and to help people to  understand that energy has always been a  social part of a social process that's  politically negotiated so as you're  asking about what some of the political  trends are I think it's important to see  that the political trends of the past  are likely to continue into the future  and so a few of those important ones one  is that the that there is a set of rules  and regulations that get set for a  dominant fuel of such as oil and that  those then help that fuel sort of get  entrenched in to the economic landscape  so examples of that might be just a  small detail of that would be the  competition between street cars and  automobiles in the city so one so this  is an interesting story because street  cars used to be the dominant  transportation but at some point cars  became more popular and and there was a  decision that the street cars would have  to give way to automobiles and  automobiles could slow down in front of  them and they could kind of clogged up  the roadway and then there was also a  decision that the street cars paid taxes  and those taxes would go to the  government which would then go to fund  the streets which would then be there to  support the automobiles and so it's a  bit of a complicated story but I think  that the main point is that rules and  regulations are strongly associated with  the dominant fuel knows will change and  I think one thing that we can expect to  see over the coming generation really is  that these rules and regulations will  change there was a article SI recently a  collection of articles recently in New  York Times that I think was kind of  interesting it was entitled uh I think  it was a tough week for coal  and it contained a series of these kinds  of rules and regulations that were being  reversed whether it was permits for  coal-fired power plants or permit the  right to dump certain kinds of mining  wastes and all those things and those  are the kinds of things are the kinds of  rules and regulations that will change  in the future okay let's continue  looking at the governmental aspect of it  do you think that states and nations can  work together to create some kind of  climate policy yeah well I mean that's a  that's really one of the most  challenging issues that society faces  today I mean if you think about at the  state level of state governments in the  United States it's clear that there are  there is an ability to collaborate and  in and actually over the last decade or  so it's really been the states that have  been leading the way and also the  localities and I think that's one of the  political insights that historians can  bring to understanding what to expect in  the future which is that climate and  energy policy are set not only at the  national level but also at the local  state and then also international level  so as to whether they can collaborate  well clearly at the state level you have  regional collaborative around greenhouse  gas emission controls right now in the  Northeast for instance and  collaborations over automobile emission  standards at the international level I  think one of the thing that one of the  lessons that historians have to offer  really has to do with how distinctive  the climate problem is from other issues  that we faced in the past and so one  example that well it guess it's how  difficult it is to find an analogy to  climate change and people like to talk  about chlorofluorocarbons and the ozone  layer and how the Montreal Protocol  solve that problem but it was really  much a much narrower issue than the  climate one which really addresses  economic development economic  development across the whole society as  opposed to a few chemicals that can be  taken out of production by a small  number of companies but at the same time  we we have had international agreements  on trade trade organizations  international  security agreements and all those have  involved States you know making  concessions and agreements and  optimistic that we'll be able to come to  an international agreement I think the  ultimate question is going to be one of  implementation and within each country  and that goes returns to my previous  comment which is just that I think it's  critical for everyone to understand that  the climate problem is one that will be  with us for generations as a political  problem that is not one that will be  solved by the passage of a climate bill  in in Congress this session but that  that will really just be the beginning  of a long period of political conflict  over the rules and institutions and  implementation of of those okay so what  are some things the government can do to  spur an energy transition in the coming  years I think the most important single  step the government can take is to find  a way to raise the price of carbon  emissions carbon dioxide and other  related get your greenhouse gas  emissions and there that that can be  justified and kind of explained by  historians if you look by looking at the  past in all the ways the government has  shaped the energy market historically  and and really acted to try to drive  down fossil fuel prices whether it's  through tax policies or property right  property kind of generous property  policies in addition to that I think  that the the government clearly has a  historic role that is played in many  industries the Internet of public  investment of public investment science  as well as public subsidies for new  technologies and a role in trying to  speed innovation and I think that if we  look historically in a series of  different industries including energy  but also other kinds of scientific  breakthroughs the government has played  a critical role in that and I think this  goes back to the the fundamental point  that I wanted to make related to the  idea of a free market energy I think  that this is one of the very problematic  myths that has grown up around the the  dominance of the fossil fuels which is  that it was purely about economic free  economic competition that resulted in  our embrace of fossil fuels to such an  extent  and I think that when you look back at  the history history this is what my  first book was about is you know you see  all the different ways the government  shaped shape the development of the oil  economy and really deepened our  dependence on fossil fuels and so I  think if we understand that the market  in the past has always been a social  market in which politics and social  decisions based on our values have  shaped that market then then we realize  that we face those same kinds of social  decisions today and that is not about  government intervening in the market but  rather making choices that we all in  evitable have to make about what that  energy market is going to look like okay  so using history as a guide in what does  the future hold for us are we going to  go the way of the dodo bird or is there  hope mmm well that's that's a little  hard to I mean I think that the most  important thing to remember is that is  that the climate change problem it's not  really one about human extinction it's  more it's more social problem that as I  was saying about that relates to our  values and the things that we care and  look care about and love and so they  might be things such as peace and  security and justice or even the special  places though that we that we that we  care about that are undergoing rapid  rapid change so I think really the the  response to climate change and this  again is where historians have something  to offer is that it's a social and  cultural problem as and political  problem as much as a technical one so  we're not going to go extinct but we  have choices to make as a society about  what kind of world we're going to live  in in the future very good thanks very  much for being here with us today and  sharing some of your work thank you very  much with pleasure for more information  about professor Saban and his research  please visit our website at Yale dot edu  backslash Macmillan report be sure to  join us again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale  you

